  Key takeaways



The browser has one thread for JavaScript and the DOM; anything expensive on it stalls painting, scrolling, and clicks.
A Web Worker is a separate thread with no shared memory — data crosses via postMessage, copied by the structured clone algorithm.
Functions and DOM nodes can never cross that boundary; only cloneable data can, unless you explicitly transfer a supported type like ArrayBuffer.
Always attach onerror, and always terminate() a worker you're done with — an uncleaned-up worker keeps running and holding memory.
Workers earn their overhead on substantial CPU-bound work, not on small or I/O-bound tasks that were never blocking the main thread to begin with.





